  "name": "read_paper",
  "arguments": {
    "url": "http://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.16300v2"
  }
}
```

Thought: The analysis of landmark tokens, hierarchical attention, and retrieval-based strategies provides a comprehensive understanding of how to extend the context length of LLMs while improving memory efficiency. These methods complement the M+ framework's latent-space memory approach. To finalize, I will synthesize the findings into a clear recommendation for experimental approaches to extend LLM context and improve memory.

Action:
```json
{
  "name": "final_answer",
  "arguments": {
    "answer": "To extend the context of LLMs and improve memory, experimental approaches like landmark tokens and retrieval-based strategies offer scalable and efficient solutions. Key recommendations include: 1) Using landmark tokens to anchor and summarize input blocks, enabling efficient retrieval and reducing computational overhead, 2) Implementing hierarchical attention mechanisms to prioritize relevant blocks while maintaining token-level access, 3) Integrating Context Miss Tokens (CMT) to dynamically signal memory retrieval needs, and 4) Employing positional augmentation to improve extrapolation to longer contexts. These methods can be combined with latent-space memory approaches, such as the M+ framework, to further enhance memory retention and scalability. While these techniques significantly improve context length (e.g., up to 160k tokens) and memory efficiency, trade-offs include potential latency from memory offloading and sensitivity to block size. These approaches are ideal for tasks requiring long-term dependencies and extended context windows."
  }
}
```

